### How does cellphone service work?

Cellular networks work off of wireless radio transmitions, the same magical power behind WiFi, Bluetooth, and radio stations.

You can compare all these wireless technologies to roads and highways.

Local roads don't go as far, and those are what WiFi and Bluetooth rely on.

Highways are for long distance travel, and Radio stations, walkie talkies, and cellular networks all go through large highways.

The highways have multiple lanes, allowing large amounts of traffic to flow.
Some of those lanes might be set aside for longer distance, higher speed, carpooling vehicles, or trucks.

There's nothing intrinsically different about those lanes, but they get set aside, and are therefore only used for those {soemthing}.

Similarly, cellular networks have multiple frequencies, each being another lane that traffic can flow through.

Each frequency can have it's own rules on how they are utilized.


### Bands

These lanes, or frequencies, are called bands.

Different carriers and regions utilitize different bands, and your phone has to be compatible with those bands.

Major smartphones companies  make variants for each region, and therefore no matter where you live or which carrier you use, you'll be able to buy an iPhone, Pixel, or Galaxy that will work for you.

Dumbphone companies, on the other hand, are generally more resource limited, and will therefore focus on specific regions.

